# TODO.md — Roadmap for v0.2 — v0.5

> **Audience: future me / future AI picking this up.**
> This document is the **handover** for completing the high-strength obfuscator.
> It is intentionally written in first-person with concrete file paths and
> design notes so that any agent (or human) can resume work without needing to
> re-read the existing code.

## What we have (v0.1 deliverable, ✅)

- Lexer (Luau 0.x subset) covering identifiers, keywords, strings (single-
  and long-bracket), numbers (dec/hex/sci), operators, comments.
- AST parser covering `local`, `if/elseif/else`, `while`, `repeat/until`,
  numeric `for`, generic `for in`, `function` (named decl + method decl),
  `return`, `do`, `break`, `continue`, expressions (arith, concat, calls,
  method calls, indexing, tables, closures).
- Three transforms actually wired into the pipeline:
  - **D1**: identifier renaming (locals → a/b/c/aa/...).
  - **D2**: numeric bitxor (`(bit32.bxor(K, N0) + (n - N0))`).
  - **D3**: string XOR (inline IIFE per string literal).
- Per-instance 32-bit cipher key derived from `mulberry32(seed)`.
- `tests/*.test.ts` cover: lexer, pipeline end-to-end, cipher round-trip.

## What's missing (and how to add it)

The original plan called for **6 stages**: parser (B), IR (C), transforms (D),
VM compiler (E), runtime template (F), emit/e2e (G), decrypt (H). Of those:

- B: **partially done**. We have working subset of Luau 0.x. Not yet in tree:
  `goto`, `::labels::`, `match`, generic-for with multiple iterators, full
  type annotations, compiler conditional expressions (`<...>`), long brackets
  inside strings (the `\` escape in `\`hello\\nworld`\`` form).
- C: **not started**. No IR.
- D1-D5: D1/D2/D3 wired; D4 (flatten) and D5 (deadcode) are stubs.
- E: **not started**. No custom bytecode VM.
- F: **not started**. No Luau-side runtime template (interpreter shell).
- G: emitter wired but minimal — no IR-based dispatch emitter.
- H: **stub only**.

The fastest path to "matches the reference" is **E + F**; without those the
rest of D4/D5 is essentially decorative.

